#tuples are ordered and immutable. They are defined using parentheses ().
#tuples can contain elements of different data types, including other tuples.
#tuples are indexed, and you can access individual elements using their index.
#tuples can be used to store related pieces of data together, such as coordinates, RGB values, or any collection of items that should not be modified.
tuple1 = (1, 2, 3, 4, 5)# data of int, float, string, list, tuple
#with tuples we can only read the data but we cannot modify it. 
# We can not add, remove or change the elements of a tuple after it has been created.
print(tuple1)
print(tuple1[0])# accessing the first element of the tuple
print(tuple1[1])# accessing the second element of the tuple
print(tuple1[0:3])
print(tuple1[0:5])

#sets are unordered and mutable. They are defined using curly braces {}.
#sets can contain elements of different data types, but they cannot contain duplicate elements.
#sets are not indexed, and you cannot access individual elements using their index. However, you can iterate over the elements of a set using a loop.
#sets are commonly used for operations like union, intersection, and difference between sets of data.
#sets are useful for storing unique items and performing mathematical set operations.

set1 = {1, 2, 3, 4, 5,5,6}# data of int, float, string, list, tuple
set2 = {4, 5, 6, 7, 8, 8} 
#with sets we can add, remove or change the elements of a set after it has been created.
print(set1)
print(1 in set1) # checking if an element is in the set
print(6 in set1) # checking if an element is in the set
for element in set1: # iterating over the elements of the set
    print(element)
# set operations
union_set = set1.union(set2) # union of two sets
intersection_set = set1.intersection(set2) # intersection of two sets
difference_set = set1.difference(set2) # difference of two sets
print("Union:", union_set)
print("Intersection:", intersection_set)
print("Difference:", difference_set)